WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.189 --> 00:00:04.540
- Thank you for your service.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.150
- Have a seat.
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.250
- I sat down, I was like,
that's not a good host.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.456
I was like, wait. Hello.

00:00:10.456 --> 00:00:12.653
- Thank you for your service.
- I know, absolutely.

00:00:12.653 --> 00:00:13.486
- Thank you so much.

00:00:13.486 --> 00:00:15.450
- And everyone watching also in service.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.283
How are y'all doing?

00:00:16.283 --> 00:00:17.630
- Good.
- Yeah.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:19.773
- Do you usually come to work
together to do something?

00:00:19.773 --> 00:00:20.637
- No.
- No.

00:00:20.637 --> 00:00:21.693
We used to. We used to.

00:00:21.693 --> 00:00:23.730
When we both used to work here at NBC.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.440
- Yeah, but it's been a minute.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.163
- It's been a few minutes.
- Yeah. A few minutes.

00:00:27.163 --> 00:00:28.580
- A few minutes. I like it.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.480
So you had some health issues,
but you're doing well, right?

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.625
- I did. I was late right
about this time last year.

00:00:34.625 --> 00:00:35.491
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:00:35.491 --> 00:00:36.720
- Yeah. I was in the hospital.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.140
I went in for one thing, ended
up having surgery for five.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.940
So it was buy one, get four free.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.310
- Oh my. That's scary.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.350
- No, it was really a
very scary time, Kelly.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.580
- Yeah.
- It was a very scary time.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.760
And Al is one who likes to look
forward, not backward. Okay.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:51.593
- The Optimist.

00:00:51.593 --> 00:00:52.710
- Which is great. Ever the optimist.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.390
But it was a very, very difficult time.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.270
And we are so blessed
to have him in full form

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.386
and just great.
- Yeah.

00:00:58.386 --> 00:00:59.709
(clapping)

00:00:59.709 --> 00:01:01.740
- But I have to say
too, your granddaughter,

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:02.790
she kind of played a role, right?

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.070
- Yes. Yeah. I found out
while I was in the hospital

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:06.646
that my oldest girl, Courtney,

00:01:06.646 --> 00:01:08.723
was expecting our first grandchild.

00:01:08.723 --> 00:01:09.556
- Aw.

00:01:09.556 --> 00:01:10.973
And there she is, little Sky.

00:01:10.973 --> 00:01:15.360
Oh, you can all go aw. I know.
(clapping)

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.420
- So special.
- But more than anything else,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.917
I think just having, knowing
that she was coming along.

00:01:20.917 --> 00:01:22.284
That she's coming-
For this last year-

00:01:22.284 --> 00:01:23.117
Exactly.

00:01:23.117 --> 00:01:24.060
- Because it's been a progression,

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.800
but you've just gotten
stronger and stronger.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:26.633
And so here he is.
- Yeah.

00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:29.280
- So now when he drops his underwear

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:30.720
and his stuff on the floor, whatever,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:31.707
and I wanna get upset with him,

00:01:31.707 --> 00:01:34.107
I'm just like, you know-
- They're there at least.

00:01:34.107 --> 00:01:36.690
- I could've been dead.
- Oh my god he's gonna use it.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:37.950
That's gonna be a card.
- Hey, hey-

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.223
- This is what you prayed for.

00:01:39.223 --> 00:01:41.700
- Hey, it's your fault.
You saved me. Sorry.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.340
- You didn't want me to go anywhere.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.680
So I have to say was it odd to you,

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:47.910
he was singing on Broadway?

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.324
That's not normally how we say
and was in Waitress, right?

00:01:50.324 --> 00:01:52.170
- I was in Waitress.
How was that first of all?

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:53.399
Did you love it? Are
you gonna do it again?

00:01:53.399 --> 00:01:55.890
- Well, I don't know if anybody
really wants to see that,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.039
but it was one of those
things where they said,

00:01:59.039 --> 00:02:01.920
my agent called and said, they
want you to be in Waitress.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.780
I said, oh, the hunky Dr. Palmer. No.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.447
They want you to play old Joe.

00:02:05.447 --> 00:02:07.050
- Cool, got it.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.250
- It's just kind of a bit of a downer.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.083
- Yeah.

00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:09.923
- But it was fun. I had a great time.

00:02:09.923 --> 00:02:11.790
- I know, I was gonna
say, was it interesting

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:12.960
to watch him in that way?

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:13.932
'Cause you don't normally.

00:02:13.932 --> 00:02:14.940
- It was, I was shocked. I was shocked.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:15.930
- Yeah.
- Because I had watched-

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.520
- Were you more nervous?

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.720
- I think she was more scared

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.100
because I was gonna make a fool on myself.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.540
- No.
- No I didn't think that.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.590
- You get one life.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.720
- And he wouldn't sing
that much for us at home.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.010
I think he was a little self-conscious.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.004
So when we saw him on
stage the first time-

00:02:28.004 --> 00:02:28.837
- Yeah.

00:02:28.837 --> 00:02:29.670
- It was the first time I saw him do it.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.440
And I actually teared up. I was like,

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.360
Al actually can sing a little bit.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:34.193
- Oh, that's so cool.
- Yeah.

00:02:34.193 --> 00:02:35.490
- So it was really a lot of fun.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.190
- And again, it was a
really nice title of a song.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.066
Take it from an old man.
- Yeah.

00:02:40.066 --> 00:02:41.757
- I know you were like doubting the role,

00:02:41.757 --> 00:02:43.290
but I actually love that.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.240
I think old Joe would
be fun. That'd be cool.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:46.978
So wait, I wanna talk
about your new documentary.

00:02:46.978 --> 00:02:48.990
- It's called, it's called Gaining Ground,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.283
the Fight for Black Land.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:55.920
It's this incredible problem
in this country where

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.170
in 1920 there was about 20 million acres

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.330
of farmland owned by black farmers.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.670
And we're down to less than 2 million now.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.100
And through different
heirs, property rights,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.560
where if one person
decides they wanna sell,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:08.790
everybody has to sell,

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:10.800
the lack of proper wills
and things like that.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.470
So we look at this through the lens of

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:14.850
how do we reverse this?

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:18.480
And we were partnered with
John Deere, the tractor folks.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.696
- Yeah. Oh, I know who they are.

00:03:19.696 --> 00:03:20.797
- Yeah.

00:03:20.797 --> 00:03:24.023
And interestingly, John
Deere was an abolitionist,

00:03:24.023 --> 00:03:25.873
you know, back in the day.
- Yeah.

00:03:25.873 --> 00:03:27.180
I don't know the backstory on John Deere.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.013
That's interesting.
- Yeah.

00:03:28.013 --> 00:03:31.710
Yeah. So, they really
tried to put in place

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:35.010
a legacy that will help going forward,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:36.630
farmers, you know, get their land.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:40.170
The interesting fact is the
fastest growing demographic

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:43.140
for new farmers, is people
of color and women, so.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.090
- I would be one of those
people 'cause I'm doing that

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:46.562
on my ranch. Yeah.
- There you go.

00:03:46.562 --> 00:03:49.290
- And it's a hard life.
- Yep.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.360
- So, but it's very fascinating.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.028
And I think everybody wants
to get back to non GMO

00:03:54.028 --> 00:03:55.650
and really healthy stuff.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.330
'Cause we all know that that's medicine.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:58.614
- Farm to table.
- Yeah.

00:03:58.614 --> 00:04:02.130
- We're what we eat literally.
- Yeah. Don't I know it.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:03.450
So two things.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:04.830
You just released a book recently.

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:05.850
- Yes.
- What is the title?

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.680
- I love this book. It's
called Lessons Learned

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.990
and Cherished, the Teacher
Who Changed My Life.

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:11.730
And it's a collection of essays from

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.140
a lot of notable people.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.300
Some of them maybe you
don't know, a lot you do.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.700
Who talk about that
teacher who just changed

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.287
something for them.
- I love that.

00:04:19.287 --> 00:04:20.907
- And it's a salute to teachers.

00:04:20.907 --> 00:04:23.621
- I'm gonna love that.
- I have a copy for you.

00:04:23.621 --> 00:04:24.705
- You left a copy for me. I know.

00:04:24.705 --> 00:04:25.980
I'm very excited.
- I'm excited for you.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:27.930
- But also congratulations,

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.580
because you were named 2020 Anchor.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.275
- Thank you. Thank you.
- Yes, this is huge.

00:04:31.275 --> 00:04:32.108
- With David Muir.
- Yeah.

00:04:32.108 --> 00:04:35.043
- I'm so excited to step in. Thank you.

00:04:35.883 --> 00:04:37.890
- You're clapping, but
you're kind of competitors.

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.860
- Not really. She gets
real news and I just-

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.000
- Oh my god do not, do not.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:42.833
- Oh my gosh.

00:04:42.833 --> 00:04:44.670
But I'm just really thrilled
because Barbara Walters,

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.214
of course started this program.

00:04:46.214 --> 00:04:47.047
- Yeah.

00:04:47.047 --> 00:04:47.880
- Along with Hugh Downs.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.980
And to be there in this
space that Barbara occupied

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:51.600
all those years after
having worked with her

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:52.433
for so many years.

00:04:52.433 --> 00:04:53.266
- I was gonna say she was a mentor, right?

00:04:53.266 --> 00:04:54.750
- She was absolutely a mentor.
- Yeah.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:55.800
- I watched and followed Barbara.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.450
I mean, she was iconic.
- Yeah.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:58.338
- I grew up watching her.
- Yeah.

00:04:58.338 --> 00:05:00.570
- So it's a great responsibility

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:01.890
and I'm just honored and humbled.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.350
Oh, you're beaming about it.
Yeah. Your eyes are smiling.

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:05.700
That's wonderful. I love that.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.687
Do you think it's gotten
better for women in news?

00:05:07.687 --> 00:05:09.060
- Oh, absolutely.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:10.170
- There always can be progress.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:12.690
- Of course. We see women in
so many different roles, right?

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:15.120
But it's more important to
see women behind the scenes.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.230
And we are starting to see,

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:17.963
but we need more women behind the scenes.

00:05:17.963 --> 00:05:18.796
- Yeah.

00:05:18.796 --> 00:05:20.910
But the industry has changed tremendously.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.220
I mean, to see so many
heads of news divisions

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.230
and so forth, it's really great to see.

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:27.870
- And especially when
it's a producing team

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:28.800
and they're producing something.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.360
Like if it's a woman
producing something about

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.382
a woman, it's like, that makes sense.

00:05:31.382 --> 00:05:32.215
- Yeah.

00:05:32.215 --> 00:05:33.048
- Yeah those are things that make sense.

00:05:33.048 --> 00:05:34.026
So how's Today going?

00:05:34.026 --> 00:05:34.859
- Everything's going great.

00:05:34.859 --> 00:05:36.270
- I just there recently.
- I know, I know.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.534
In fact, the head of the Today brand,

00:05:38.534 --> 00:05:42.474
Libby Least our Uber producer.

00:05:42.474 --> 00:05:44.400
- You know, we have a great time.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.860
It's just a really good family.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.100
- You have a great crew.
- Hoda, Carson, Craig, Chanel.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.197
Chanel Jones ran the
New York City marathon.

00:05:52.197 --> 00:05:53.030
- Oh my gosh.

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:54.900
- For the first time. Did a fantastic job.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.606
- That was who I was
waving at from my house

00:05:57.606 --> 00:06:00.003
because I was like, never me.

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:03.210
- Al ran it. Al ran it years ago.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.085
- You did?
- I did. I did.

00:06:05.085 --> 00:06:08.100
- There's too many things
that can go wrong with chafing

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.770
and people I hear have
to go to the bathroom

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:13.440
and I'm like, and they just go.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.690
- I had chafed nipples.
- Oh.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:17.790
I'm just not about that life.

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:19.410
But I'm so proud of people that do it.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.466
- I'll give you a sense
of how fast I ran it.

00:06:21.466 --> 00:06:24.510
Our daughter Lela said at
the end, she said, daddy,

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:26.100
what did the snail say on top of the turf?

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.600
- Oh no.
- I don't know. Wee.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:30.330
- But you're like, well,
what did the snail say

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:31.260
when he finished something?

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:32.451
- That's right.

00:06:32.451 --> 00:06:34.530
- You have covered,
both of you have covered

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:35.550
so many military stories.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.320
Is there one that sticks
out to you, Deborah?

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:40.650
- I remember prior to
the nine eleven attack,

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:43.193
we had another attack on US
soil the very, very first time.

00:06:43.193 --> 00:06:46.980
And I did a story up in
Massachusetts about a young woman

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:48.720
who was getting ready to go to college,

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.910
who decided she wanted to
go and serve her country.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.130
She was called to action into duty.

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.290
And there were a number of
people who were deciding,

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.030
I want to serve my country.
- Yeah.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:59.490
- And I remember just
being so in awe of that,

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:00.795
that whole spirit of wanting to serve.

00:07:00.795 --> 00:07:02.220
- Selflessness.
- Selflessness.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.260
So that was actually one
that I will never forget.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:05.860
I was really very impressed by that woman.

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:07.230
- Yeah. Called to a higher purpose.

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:09.060
That's really, really
beautiful. What about you?

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.490
- You know, I had read about this story,

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.664
it was the battalion,
the six triple eight.

00:07:14.664 --> 00:07:18.801
It was the only battalion
of all women of color

00:07:18.801 --> 00:07:20.652
who were sent overseas-

00:07:20.652 --> 00:07:22.184
(applause)

00:07:22.184 --> 00:07:25.770
they were the six triple
eight coastal battalion.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:29.340
There was a backlog of about
18 months mail to and from,

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.410
from the front lines that could not get.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:34.800
And they were led by the
first black female colonel.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.480
They went over first to England,

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:38.640
then behind the enemy lines in France.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.610
And within six months had the mail service

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:42.690
running to and from.

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.970
And I had the pleasure
and honor of interviewing

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:49.530
Ms. Lena King, who is still
alive. She's 101 years old.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:50.856
- Dang.
- Yes.

00:07:50.856 --> 00:07:53.460
(applause)

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:58.460
There were 855 of these women,
only six are still alive.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.870
And I got to meet one other,

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:02.430
but also got to interview Ms. King.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:03.990
She's still sharp as a tack.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:07.031
And so one of the things she said was,

00:08:07.031 --> 00:08:09.990
we wanted to show that
we loved our country,

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:12.390
even though our country
didn't always love us back.

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:15.090
- Yeah. Well that's real love. Yeah.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:17.490
I love what the Today Show did too.

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:19.380
Y'all actually lobbied for
these women to be recognized.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:20.430
- That's right.

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:22.320
They got their Congressional
medal of honor.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:27.320
Many of them posthumously.
(applause)

00:08:27.683 --> 00:08:30.750
It really meant, it meant
a lot. It meant a lot.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:33.283
- And it does matter.
Getting those letters matter.

00:08:33.283 --> 00:08:35.220
That's really important.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:36.270
- Before emails.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:38.640
- Yeah, absolutely. That's
what I'm saying, yeah.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:40.230
This is, it's even more important.

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:42.480
Well, everybody, you can
see Al on the Today Show

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.670
every morning on NBC if you
didn't already know that.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:46.710
And also in the third hour of today.

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:50.133
And also watch Deborah Friday
evenings on 2020 on ABC.

